      "math_academy_id": "q-81327",
      "knowledge_point_id": "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors",
      "problem": "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=-i+j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and $j.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=-i+j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/cee26b17d477344e138c3d6d9228f23d67c4c78ce447b1c2c3a887b79125ee7a.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a horizontal shear with a shear factor of $-1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
