    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-81413",
      "knowledge_point_id": "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/72c88d1df90c3462d7f1f186c06b9a7acf30c071f5ba8bbf722ee125fee0e616.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the rectangle shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
