  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5450",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image",
      "problem": "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/0adb71786ea5de6203b155882b20000488d5467cf6dfcd34e6fa4e06ec091e46.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/94fb0fd9708c343c89fd98ff5b87c901c9e795026cc1dbd35d29cff92d6fe59e.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we are told that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ is $4$ times the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{v},$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/779a10caf7c07cbe4b5891240892db5433adc0f9f8895192a3a8bed5f9301126.png)",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5454",
      "knowledge_point_id": "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors",
      "problem": "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j},$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/1e5310f50dab35bc25447af9b37d972e11eda0afee98ac99b87a819ba62f56a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5455",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix",
      "problem": "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/e335abcb649dae3ed9ade01017a23998614c3d0ff5a0898eb9b4d657f68e8b62.png)",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of  $\\mathbf T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $2.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5456",
      "knowledge_point_id": "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6231eb09a75376d694a6f47c47cf5a26aab4a32c28b2799f7e1f0825a7017f88.png) be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Looking at the matrix $T,$ we can see that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear of shear factor $-1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/2d3d15c4d6b6f9e08c6fbcc35236b50dcd6cf4d09e9204e0aa255706461c3dd8.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
